  context="In 214 BC, during Rome's great war with Carthage, Syracuse went over to the Carthaginian side. Rome sent the general Marcus Claudius Marcellus to take it, by land and by sea. Polybius wrote this about sixty years later, from earlier accounts. This is Evelyn Shuckburgh's translation of 1889; his section numbers and two footnote numbers are left out, and the description of the Romans' ship-borne ladders (VIII.6) is cut.",
  plain="The Romans expected to take Syracuse in five days. They forgot Archimedes. He had built catapults for every range, holes in the walls for archers, cranes that dropped huge stones on the Roman ships, and an iron hand that lifted a ship by its bow and dropped it. Marcellus joked about it, but gave up the assault. Polybius's lesson: one mind, properly prepared, can be worth an army.",
