   context="In 75 BC Cicero served as quaestor, a junior financial officer, in Sicily. Thirty years later, in a book on whether virtue is enough for happiness, he contrasts the cruel tyrant Dionysius of Syracuse with the 'humble' mathematician of the same city, and remembers finding his grave. This is Yonge's translation.",
   plain="Cicero says that when he was in Sicily the people of Syracuse did not know where Archimedes was buried and even denied the tomb existed. Cicero remembered that it had a sphere and a cylinder on it. Searching among the tombs at one of the city gates, he saw a small column with that shape above the brambles. Men with scythes cleared the way, and he found the half-worn verses. So a Roman from a country town had to show the Greeks where their greatest genius lay. Who, Cicero asks, would not rather be that mathematician than the tyrant?",
